WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.090
- I was just looking for
that next great idea,

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:06.470
that's where the whole
slew of origin stories

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:07.303
kind of started.

00:00:07.303 --> 00:00:11.500
So I made a list of historical
and fictional characters,

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.040
and you could find names
like Joan of Arc or Napoleon

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.370
or Dracula or whatever.

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:20.397
And I got to Santa Claus
and I said, "Huh...

00:00:20.397 --> 00:00:22.564
"Nah," and I just moved on.

00:00:22.564 --> 00:00:25.147
(gentle music)

00:00:29.992 --> 00:00:32.275
- [Klaus] Ho ho ho!

00:00:32.275 --> 00:00:33.520
(Klaus laughing)
- Wait wait wait.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:34.930
Time out, really?

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:35.780
That's how you laugh?

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.841
- When I thought Santa Claus origin story,

00:00:37.841 --> 00:00:40.140
I was like, "I'm not
sure I wanna do that."

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.470
But then once you say
there's something there.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:47.510
Let me see if I can find that
non-corny version of this.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:48.537
(snowball thuds)
- Ow!

00:00:48.537 --> 00:00:50.563
- (laughs) Ho ho, you loser!

00:00:50.563 --> 00:00:52.140
- Oh yeah, well, if I'm a loser,

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.827
then you're a, you're a...

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.030
You're too far, you're gone.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.990
- I try to have a kernel of truth

00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.930
underneath all this fantasy,

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:04.050
and found this list of
the human settlements,

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.840
the Northern most human
settlements in history.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.220
In the 1600s there was this
place called Smerenburg,

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:10.980
without the S,

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:15.980
and it used to be a Dutch
and Danish whaling post.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.023
- There she is, Smerensburg.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:25.630
- I thought, if you hold
off the magic, hold it off.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:28.050
- What happened here?

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.830
- Oh, colorful local traditions,

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.620
quirky folklore, that kind of thing.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:36.300
All part of this quaint
town's vibrant culture.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.610
- 'Cause this is a prequel to your Santa,

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.120
how much can we hold off on the magic

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.910
until that moment comes across?

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:45.670
You know, it has to come in.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:48.532
And I thought that was the fun of it.

00:01:48.532 --> 00:01:51.032
(child gasps)

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:53.487
(child gasps)

00:01:53.487 --> 00:01:56.106
(reindeer whooshing)

00:01:56.106 --> 00:01:57.580
(sleigh screeches)

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:00.090
Magic in film can't be a cheapskate.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.840
That's why things like
"Harry Potter" work,

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.983
'cause there's rules to everything.

00:02:04.983 --> 00:02:07.500
- He's huge, but he can
squeeze on any chimney.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.320
- Really, how?
- I don't know.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:10.415
Magic, I guess.

00:02:10.415 --> 00:02:11.356
(Jesper thuds)
- Ow!

00:02:11.356 --> 00:02:13.439
(Jesper thuds)
Ah!

00:02:13.439 --> 00:02:15.000
(chimney whirring)

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.510
- I am at heart a traditional animator.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.560
I started in traditional animation

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.800
when CGI was just starting out,

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.640
but I got into animation
because I love to draw.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.310
When we kinda click at
your drawings to come alive

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:32.190
and to feel and to think,

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.020
that's the ultimate form of drawing,

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.670
and that's what got me into animation.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.620
So when CGI came along, I embraced it,

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.520
I thought it was more
of a split in the road.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.360
I didn't quite realize
that it was being looked at

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.200
as some evolution,

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.960
and I never really agreed with
the reasons that I was given

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.630
for that decision.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:55.080
We'd been in a trend in CGI where

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:00.910
every advancement was uninspiring

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:02.670
and realism seemed unattainable.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.180
So then we got to the point
where now it looks real,

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.880
and now we're bored. (laughs)

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.240
We wanted all of the elements,

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.110
no matter where they came
from in the pipeline,

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.850
to feel like they were
painted by one single hand,

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:17.550
and the people that need
to do this lighting system

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.603
are people who know to paint light.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:24.210
The bulk of the team was a very young crew

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:27.267
of kids out of school

00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:32.030
that most of them not had
one single professional job,

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:34.300
and all of a sudden you put
in them to work on something

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:37.100
where the bar is this high, right?

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:42.100
It was interesting to
see 45 to 60-year-olds,

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:48.100
and then 20 to 27-year-olds,
and nothing in the middle.

00:03:48.456 --> 00:03:51.260
That's where the CGI bomb exploded

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:54.620
and nobody got trained into
animation for all that time.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.980
But there's a resurgence
in the interest by students

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.730
that wanna learn that
technique, and it's great.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.160
I felt like I create the environment

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:04.063
in which I would love to work at myself.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.660
We got really luck, we had
great casting directors.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:11.000
They sent a list of their proposals

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.140
for the character of Klaus.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:14.607
On the top of the list was J.K. Simmons,

00:04:14.607 --> 00:04:17.480
and I just put it down and
said, "Yes, please." (laughs)

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.570
I didn't even look at the others.

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:22.857
And he said yes, so a
week later they told me,

00:04:22.857 --> 00:04:23.690
"Yeah, he's in."

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:24.970
I'm like, "Really?"

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.460
And J.K. was a true professional.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:31.790
He did that whole monologue
of him telling his backstory,

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:33.650
and he did the whole thing in one take,

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:36.550
and we were in tears.

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:37.417
We were in the booth going,

00:04:37.417 --> 00:04:40.290
"Yes, that's pretty good." (laughs)

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.060
It was amazing.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.350
And then Rashida was a godsend

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.040
because she's a writer herself,

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:49.927
and she would say, "I'm not sure

00:04:49.927 --> 00:04:50.883
"that's how Alva would say it,"

00:04:50.883 --> 00:04:53.900
or, "I'm not sure Alva
would even say that at all."

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.547
And, man, I let her school me.

00:04:56.547 --> 00:04:59.880
And then she improved almost every line.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.350
Norm was fantastic, too.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:06.350
Norm was just such a great, honest, timid,

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.340
you wouldn't imagine, timid guy.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:11.940
But then once we'd start rolling,

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:16.851
he became this larger-than-life
sarcastic boatman.

00:05:16.851 --> 00:05:19.370
(Mogens whistling)

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:21.050
- Is the weather always like this.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:23.760
- Eh, we're having a bit of a heat wave.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.760
- The one that was the hardest
to figure out was Jesper.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.440
- Jesper Johanssen, postman.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:33.370
We were never properly...

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:34.926
(Klaus grunts)
(wood creaks)

00:05:34.926 --> 00:05:35.759
(Jesper gulps)

00:05:35.759 --> 00:05:37.560
- There's a real danger

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.690
when you have a character like Jesper

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:41.457
that he might become
unlikable to the audience.

00:05:41.457 --> 00:05:43.740
And if people decide,
"I don't like this guy,"

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:44.590
they shut the door

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:46.260
and they don't go on
this journey with him,

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.040
then you don't have a story.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:49.653
But you still have to make him,

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:52.970
you know, a bit of a jerk.

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:54.240
- Forgive me, I'm kind of a dreamer,

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.510
but isn't that why we do it?

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:57.994
Hm hm?

00:05:57.994 --> 00:05:59.370
(Alva grunts)

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:02.190
- So you need someone who can pull it off,

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:05.830
and Jason turned out to be
the perfect guy for that

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:07.770
'cause even when he's
doing horrible things,

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:09.440
you still like him.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:10.490
He loves to improvise.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:12.017
He did this thing with
a little kid's voice.

00:06:12.017 --> 00:06:14.760
"Remember when I would
help you with the letters?"

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.290
- Daddy, can I help you mail the letters?

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:19.673
Can I help you put the
medals in the letters

00:06:19.673 --> 00:06:21.597
then put them in a stamp with the,

00:06:21.597 --> 00:06:23.242
wait wait wait wait wait!

00:06:23.242 --> 00:06:24.900
- We just cracked up, and it was like,

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.350
and it became such an iconic
moment for the character.

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:33.090
- A true selfless act
always sparks another.

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:35.290
- A true selfless act
always sparks another.

00:06:36.645 --> 00:06:39.562
The whole film is just to convey

00:06:39.562 --> 00:06:42.330
the most poetic version of that message.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:43.500
It is true.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:44.820
If someone holds a door for you,

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.280
you like to hold a door for someone else.

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.310
It's just human nature,

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:50.473
and it's good to remind people of that.

